Id: fsys004a
Severity: ALERT
Category: filesystem
Check: check_suid
Fix: chmod u-s the file, unless its package installs it so (a vendor's, which Tigris cannot check on dpkg: then accept it, tigris-accept).
References: chmod(1)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST AC-6, CM-7; ISO 8.2, 8.18; CE Secure configuration

The program is setuid, and no installed package makes it so. Tigris
compares with the package manager: rpm's and apk's file modes and
pacman's mtree files; on dpkg systems, which record no modes, the
installed packages' file lists against Tigris's list of what Debian
and Ubuntu packages make setuid (made from Debian's archive and from
installing packages, util/mkdebspecial), and the modes
dpkg-statoverride records. Elsewhere Tigris's own list for the
platform. On dpkg the detail names the package the file is from: a
vendor's package Tigris's list does not know (a browser's
chrome-sandbox) may ship it setuid, while a file no package installed
was put there by hand. One finding per file.
